		<div class="long">
			<div class="head">
									<div class="options">
						<a href="javascript:;" onclick="togglePostFold(event,4188997)" class="btn btn-default btn-small btn-sm">Fold</a>
					</div>
								<div class="title" id="post-title-4188997">
																		
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/greenwolf" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1814684); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1814684&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1605928508" alt="GreenWolf" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1814684)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/greenwolf" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1814684); return false;" >GreenWolf</a></span> <span class="odate time_1552925894 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">18 Mar 2019 16:18</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4188997">
									

<p>This is a bad idea. By lowering the barrier to entry for making CSS themes this far, this tool is going to lead to a deluge of low effort themes by people who do not have the knowledge or inclination to maintain them, and will exhaust the resources of tech team &#8212; as it is, <em>most of the themes on site</em> are in violation of the current CSS policy, specifically the prohibition on hotlinking assets, and I can't imagine that this will improve that situation.</p>
<p>To be completely and frankly honest, the last thing this site needs is more CSS themes &#8212; and speaking as the maintainer of several major themes, and one of the people indirectly responsible for the current prevalence of themes, I would even go so far as to advocate <em>the complete removal of CSS themes from the site</em>. (Although I will leave that debate for a different thread.)</p>
<p>To paraphrase Jeff Goldblum, you spent too much time wondering whether you could, and not enough wondering whether you should.</p>

							</div>

								
					
