				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/the-great-hippo" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(3938622); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=3938622&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1605928508" alt="The Great Hippo" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=3938622)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/the-great-hippo" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(3938622); return false;" >The Great Hippo</a></span> <span class="odate time_1552928785 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">18 Mar 2019 17:06</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4189042">
									

<p>While I agree that having everyone and their grandmom produce their own CSS theme is not an ideal situation (and I weep for the site's tech team in having to deal with the possibility of every author page having its own CSS theme, nevermind the inevitable CSS theme for CSS theme pages), I cannot find it in me to oppose the notion of providing access to resources that help people better understand how theming (and CSS) works.</p>
<p>Yes, I get it; a little information is a dangerous thing. But if CSS themes really do run the risk of breaking the site (literally or just aesthetically), they should be something that requires administrator approval <em>anyway</em>. Trying to be cagey about how much information we provide authors with runs against the spirit of collaborative work: we should strive to help authors achieve the most autonomy possible. That means trusting them with every tool we can that helps them achieve their vision and add it to the site (so long as it doesn't <em>break</em> the site).</p>

							</div>

								
