			<div class="content" id="post-content-4189121">
									

<blockquote>
<p>Do you also think CSS themes should be bloated and uncommented messes? Or we should actively hide the code of themes on site, to prevent them from being modified?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>While I disagree with GW's sentiment, I think this is an unfair dig: He's not saying we should make this harder. He's just opposed to making it easier.</p>
<p>To go one step further, I'll even argue that case a little: You don't install a toddler chair in a driver's seat, because you don't want to make cars easier for toddlers to drive. That doesn't mean you want to make cars harder for <em>everyone else</em>; you just don't want people driving cars unless they can learn to do so safely. You don't trust toddlers with this.</p>
<p>Where I disagree is that I <em>do</em> trust authors to create their own themes &#8212; even newer authors with little to no CSS experience.</p>
<p>(I also acknowledge that the tech team may have some serious concerns about this on the technical end, though.)</p>

							</div>

								
					
