				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/the-great-hippo" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(3938622); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=3938622&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1605928508" alt="The Great Hippo" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=3938622)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/the-great-hippo" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(3938622); return false;" >The Great Hippo</a></span> <span class="odate time_1552935188 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">18 Mar 2019 18:53</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4189134">
									

<p>There's actually a really good analog here in a recent program I've encountered: <a href="https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grammarly">Grammarly</a>.</p>
<p>I fucking hate Grammarly. It allows writers with a poor understanding of grammar to approximate a better understanding of grammar without obtaining that understanding through actual understanding. The amount of times I've found a piece correct grammatically &#8212; but wrong structurally and compositionally &#8212; only to discover Grammarly was the culprit? Well, it's currently more than five.</p>
<p>But the thing is &#8212; as much as I hate Grammarly, it leads to more writers producing more content that is grammatically sound. This produces weird gaps of competence, but it also means <em>more people writing</em>. And while I'll continue to argue that mastery over grammar is superior to reliance on a program, I think having more people willing to try things in the first place (because now it's more accessible) is, ultimately, a good thing for any artistic medium. Be it writing or CSS theming.</p>

							</div>

								
