					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/modernmajorgeneral" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(274283); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=274283&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1605928508" alt="ModernMajorGeneral" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=274283)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/modernmajorgeneral" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(274283); return false;" >ModernMajorGeneral</a></span> <span class="odate time_1552947232 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">18 Mar 2019 22:13</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4189271">
									

<p>The difference between increasing the number of people writing and the number of people making CSS themes is that this is a writing site and not a CSS site - it has established systems in place to filter out badly written articles, official channels and resources to help writers improve, a culture of some reasonable expectations around writing quality, and a large proportion of the active userbase has <em>some</em> knowledge of how to write, even the non-writers, and provides feedback on occasion.</p>
<p>None of these apply for CSS themes. In theory, you can quality control CSS by downvoting articles with bad CSS and leaving feedback on article pages, but the expectations around this are murky, and I'm not comfortable downvoting good articles with bad CSS anyway. In this situation, frankly, I'm not going to feel particularly bad about gatekeeping the number of people producing CSS themes.</p>
<p><em>However</em>, I think implying this tool will lead to a million people making low effort CSS themes is unfair; considering people don't read the guides, and this still takes some effort to use, the people who will find and use this are going to have an interest in creating CSS themes already.</p>

							</div>

								
					
