															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/decibelles" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2005044); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2005044&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1605928508" alt="Decibelles" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2005044)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/decibelles" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2005044); return false;" >Decibelles</a></span> <span class="odate time_1552955604 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">19 Mar 2019 00:33</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4189361">
									

<p>Alright, I'll rephrase what I said to actually cover what I mean and avoid using the wrong semantics: literally anyone who is able to access this page and operate its utilities and functions can make a theme, much in the same way that literally anyone can use one of the SCP sandboxes provided they are able to access those and use its utilities and functions, or that literally anyone can read the writing guides. That doesn't mean that <em>literally anyone</em> is capable of making a theme, or writing something for the site, it just means that the tool is there.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I've read the instructions on this page about four different times today, consulting with Wikidot's syntax pages as well, and I'm still unable to use this tool for jackshit because I'm not capable of making a theme right now. Maybe that just means I'm not smart enough to use the page, or the page instructions are a bunch of nonsense<sup class="footnoteref"><a id="footnoteref-729505-1" href="javascript:;" class="footnoteref" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnote-729505-1')">1</a></sup>, but not literally anyone can make a theme with this. I can't.</p>
<p>If people are unavailable to use the tools you give them, either literally or properly, then that doesn't mean giving them the tools was a bad idea; it just means they can't use it. We have a bunch of writing guides and essays on the site, especially ones people are told that they are <em>actually literally required to read</em> in order to join the site.<sup class="footnoteref"><a id="footnoteref-729505-2" href="javascript:;" class="footnoteref" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnote-729505-2')">2</a></sup> The inevitability of it is that some people don't, and they either interpret said information wrong when they write or just aren't able to write something that meets the quality standards of the site. That doesn't mean the tools are at fault, even though anyone can write a thing, much like anyone can click buttons and type in values in the tool.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Getting into subjective territory here</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Aside from preemptively and massively devaluing any themes people make because of this tool, good or bad, the only point in this paragraph that seems relevant is that a probable result coming from this tool would be &quot;a geocities-esque Web 1.0 design disaster&quot;. A fair viewpoint, but if a theme complies with the CSS Policy and still seems bad, downvoting and (optionally) leaving your thoughts on it still seems like the way to go. Hopefully, the Deletions Policy will cover CSS themes that reach -10, and if they don't already, staff should seriously consider having themes fall under said policy. At that point, I don't see the issue with the subjective side of CSS themes (again, so long as they fall under the policy).</p>
<blockquote>
<p>And we haven't even discussed the maintenance issues this will inevitably lead to.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I agree with a lot of this paragraph! However, it reads to me like it assumes that anyone who makes a theme, whether on their own or with this tool, would not ask a more experienced user to help them fix any issues or implement any changes, and instead will just knowingly leave their theme to rot. That seems like a really uncharitable view to default to for creators on this site.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p><em>Long post collapsed. ~Zyn</em></p>
<p>EDIT: Also, tangentially, to cover a broad point you make in this thread, so as to not reply to you countlessly in order to make a response to said point: making CSS themes easier and more accessible to make isn't a bad thing, and taking the default position of assuming that it'll lead to nothing but bad or broken themes is very pessimistic of both the userbase and their potential to create.</p>
<div class="footnotes-footer">
<div class="title">Footnotes</div>
<div class="footnote-footer" id="footnote-729505-1"><a href="javascript:;" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnoteref-729505-1')">1</a>. Doubtful; I'm not smart, nor a coder, so anything I can't understand from the page I assume to be due to my own shortcomings.</div>
<div class="footnote-footer" id="footnote-729505-2"><a href="javascript:;" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnoteref-729505-2')">2</a>. And yes, debates over the existence and/or usefulness of writing essays/non-required writing guides is one people can and do have, but that's not something I wish to get into as said debates are irrelevant to my point.</div>
</div>

