					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/stormbreath" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(3075960); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=3075960&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1605928508" alt="stormbreath" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=3075960)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/stormbreath" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(3075960); return false;" >stormbreath</a></span> <span class="odate time_1552938936 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">18 Mar 2019 19:55</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4189182">
									

<p>Perhaps I wasn't clear enough when I said &quot;extremely minimal&quot;. What I meant by that was knowing the following CSS terms: <tt>color, border, background, box-shadow, filter, !important</tt>. The only one of those terms that this tool doesn't rely on an understanding of is !important. An author who wants to make a theme can easily copy the code and simply change those terms as appropriate. This is only automating a process.</p>
<p>The reason I said &quot;extremely minimal&quot; rather than &quot;basically none&quot; was a realization that users had to know that <tt>color</tt> referred to text-color, rather than anything else.</p>

							</div>

